Z||000008||SKIDMARK||12-26-2001||12:42 AM||||More details please. You may not be getting enough fuel for some reason. If the engine temp sensor is going bad, the computer thinks the engine is warm and won't enrich the mixture. This may or may not trip the check engine light or set a code. Also low fuel pressure from a bad pump is a possiblity. An extreme vacuum leak will cause a no-start. Fouled plugs, weak spark, plugged converter are all things to check. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||stingant0||12-26-2001||02:26 PM||||I dont have a cat, have a new fuel pump 40 psi...the spark plugs are new....the car did start but ran really rough then died...started it again and shot 4ft flames!!! never started after that...just keeps cranking, somtimes it wants to start. Tried forcing it by push starting it but....nothing.<P>My brother broke the door latch by pulling on it...broke the lock cylinder twice in the winter...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fat Frank||12-28-2001||10:02 AM||||You might want to check the plug wires to make sure the order is right.If they are not right it could be firing a cylinder when the intake valve is open. That will give you some fire out of the intake. Also if it does start it will run very rough.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fat Frank||12-28-2001||10:11 AM||||You might want to check the plug wiring order.If the wires are out of place it will run very rough,if at all and will sometimes backfire thru the intake.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Turbo88||12-28-2001||10:36 AM||||If you shot 4 foot flames out of your exhaust it could be your firing order is off, too much fuel, or a bad map sensor(?). I'm assuming sinse you said you have compression on all cylinders that your cam is turning. Otherwise some of them wouldn't have any compression. Also, if your cam wasn't turning, you would more than likely have a cylinder firing back up into the intake. You would notice this rapidly! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> My 2.8 ran on 4 cylinders with #1 and #3 when I first brought it back to life. I didn't see anywhere you mentioned what engine this is but try these things..<P>A. Try to crank it a few times but not too many. If you shot a big flame out then *I* would guess there is a lot of fuel in a few cylinders and it ignited. Fuel sitting in the cylinders for a while will desolve the oil off your cylinder walls and could wear the rings out. Once you crank it a few times, pull a couple plugs to see if they are wet. If they are, you've probably flooded it. If you have, remove all your plugs, unplug your fuel pump relay and crank the engine. That will dry your cylinders. reinstall the plugs, double check firing order and try to crank it off. <P>B. If none of your plugs are wet, double check that your plugwires are installed correctly. The firing order is 1-2-3-4-5-6. (Gotta love that!). As a point of reference for anyone that doesn't know, the number one cylinder is on the far passenger side rear on a 2.8lt. Number two is on the other bank far passenger side front. I don't know the details on a 4 banger. <P>C. I would consider changing the MAP sensor. Check all your wiring. If your plugs are dry, double check that you have connectivity to all your injectors. <P>I hope this helps, let us know what it ends up being.. Good Luck!<P>||||reg||1
